{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"end-of-tenancy-track","kind":"workflow","title":"End of tenancy track","description":"Takes a tenant from deciding to leave to getting the deposit back in gated steps - a valid notice, check-out preparation, the handover day, then deposit follow-up and any dispute.","category":"tenancy","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","verify","ship","review"],"role":["individual","student","parent"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["document","text","notes"],"output":["plan","checklist","message"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["moving-out","deposit","notice-period","check-out-inspection","tenant-rights"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-tenant-notice-to-leave","prepare-for-tenancy-check-out","deep-clean-for-move-out","demand-deposit-return"],"personas":["tenant-rights-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"tenancy","description":"Tenancy start date, fixed term or rolling, rent and payment date, notice clause, who is on the lease, the deposit amount and where it is held, and whether you have a check-in inventory.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"plan","description":"When you want to leave, where you are moving, and anything already said to the landlord.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Country and state or region. Notice rules, deposit protection and dispute routes vary.","type":"string","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"notice","file":"steps/01-notice.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"check-out-prep","file":"steps/02-check-out-prep.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/03-handover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve"},{"id":"deposit","file":"steps/04-deposit.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Ends one tenancy cleanly, the way a tenant adviser would want it done: a notice that actually ends the tenancy on the intended date, a home returned in a state the check-in record supports, a handover with proof of everything, and a deposit chased through the proper route if it does not come back.\n\n<tenancy>\n{{tenancy}}\n</tenancy>\n\n<plan>\n{{plan}}\n</plan>\n\nLocation: {{country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nEach step produces its outputs and a gate checklist, then stops for the tenant's approval. The tenant may come back weeks later; pick up at the step they name. Later steps depend on real events (the landlord's reply, the check-out report, the deposit outcome): ask for them and never invent them. Mark every notice period, deposit deadline and dispute rule for {{country}} `[CHECK locally]` rather than stating it as fact, and point to a tenant advice service when money or the end date is in doubt. If the tenant asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining planning steps in one reply and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"notice","file":"steps/01-notice.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Notice\n\nMake sure the tenancy ends on the date the tenant intends.\n\n1. Work out the earliest valid end date from the lease and the likely local rules, showing the calculation and marking legal minimums `[CHECK locally]`. If the tenancy is in a fixed term without a break clause, say notice may not end it early and outline the options (surrender by agreement, replacement tenant).\n2. If joint tenants are staying, warn that one tenant's notice may affect everyone and recommend advice first.\n3. Draft the notice letter with the end date, forwarding address, request for a check-out appointment, and signatures of all leaving tenants.\n4. Explain how to serve it with proof.\n5. Gate checklist: end date confirmed, notice sent with proof, landlord's acknowledgement requested, rent payable until the end date diarised.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"check-out-prep","file":"steps/02-check-out-prep.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Check-out preparation\n\nReturn the home in a condition the check-in record supports.\n\n1. Ask for the check-in inventory or move-in photos and the current condition room by room, and stop if neither is given.\n2. Compare item by item and classify each as likely fair wear and tear, likely damage, an alteration to undo, or already present at check-in.\n3. List repairs worth doing (cheaper than a likely deduction, done properly) and items to leave alone.\n4. Plan the clean at the level recorded at check-in, pointing to a move-out cleaning plan for the detail.\n5. Gate checklist: repairs booked or done, alterations undone or agreed in writing, cleaning planned, belongings and rubbish removal arranged.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/03-handover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Handover day\n\nLeave with proof of everything.\n\n1. Evidence: dated photos and video of every room and item on the inventory, close-ups matching check-in photos, meter readings photographed.\n2. Accounts: utilities, internet and local taxes closed or transferred from the end date, post redirected.\n3. Check-out: attend if possible, walk through with the inventory, note disagreements on the day, ask for a copy of the report.\n4. Keys: return every key and fob, get a written receipt with date and time.\n5. Send a short message confirming the handover, the forwarding address and a request that any proposed deductions come in writing with evidence.\n6. Gate checklist: evidence saved in two places, keys receipt held, accounts closed, confirmation message sent.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"deposit","file":"steps/04-deposit.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 4: Deposit follow-up\n\nGet the deposit back, or contest deductions through the right route.\n\n1. Ask for the landlord's response: full return, proposed deductions with evidence, or silence. Do not assume one.\n2. If deductions are proposed, assess each against the check-in record, the tenancy length and the evidence, and classify it as accept, negotiate or dispute, with reasons.\n3. Draft a reply: accepting what is fair, contesting the rest with evidence, proposing a figure, and asking for the undisputed balance now.\n4. If there is no reply or no agreement, outline the dispute routes to check locally `[CHECK locally]`: the deposit protection scheme's dispute service, a deposit authority, a tribunal or small-claims court, and any penalties for unprotected deposits, with the time limits to confirm.\n5. Final checklist: deadlines diarised, evidence pack ready, copies of all correspondence kept."}]}
